{
  "id": "kaseko_guyane",
  "name": "Kasékò (French Guianese Creole drum-dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Kasékò is the principal Creole drum-dance genre of French Guiana, centred on Cayenne. A long drum laid on the ground is played with the hands while sticks (tibwa) beat on wood and shakers (chacha) keep the pulse, under a lead singer answered by a chorus of dancers. It shares its name, usually glossed from French 'casser le corps', with the Surinamese kaseko band genre but is a distinct drum-and-song form. The membrane-drum card stands in for the kasékò drum and the palitos card for the tibwa.",
  "recipe": "classic modern kasékò, groove Percussion, Caribbean branch. membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, palitos, shaker source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
